I understand that, especially in this win-happy age.  Most people play
RPGs (of whatever type) with an eye towards winning.

I'm thinking, specifically, about an older way of playing games, things
like Rogue which really were almost impossible to win.  Because of
this, a scoreboard was an essential component of Rogue.

While from our perspective Nethack is a lot easier to win than Rogue
was, to a new player it looks almost exactly the same, that is, like an
insurmountable wall.

> I think the game itself
> gives plenty of feedback on how well you did. Most of us in our
> pre-ascension days probably rate it by how far down in the dungeon we
got.

I'm not so sure, the player has nothing to readily compare it to.
Scores can at least be compared to scores already on the board, so if
he's playing solo he can readily compare to past performance, and in a
multi-user setup he can look at how others did.

Further, if you dive it's not uncommon to get down several levels
before the monster generation catches up with you.

> The point
at which the system breaks down is for advanced  players, who can
create
> an arbitrarily high score with little risk by arbitrarily prolonging
the
> midgame (regardless of whether they ascend or not).

This is just because that's the way the game is currently, and could
perhaps be fixed with a scoring overhaul.  Maybe by weighting score
awards for monsters depending on player level, or decreasing it, like
in the old days, according to the number of monsters killed of each
type.

> That's based on the patch I mentioned above.

Ah, I thought you were referring to a proposed patch, not an actual
one.  Cool.

To speak against my own point for a moment....

I can't help but think that people (including myself) wouldn't be
complaining of this if the score table over at alt.org didn't have a
two billion point score at the top of the list.  The fixing of the
scoreboard is basically a fix with an eye towards the public servers,
in a way.

Ultimately, I like score in Nethack because I'm a score junkie.  I
blame pinball for this.  I actually play arcade games for points.
Maybe this is idiosyncratic to me, but I can't be the only one who
likes a big, weighty number behind my performance.  Big numbers are
cool!  But that number is a lot less cool if it's meaningless, if it's
too arbitrary in origin.

